{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`scripts/mutation_gate.py` isolates the **source tree** (`git archive` into a temp dir)\nbut not **runtime effects**. On 2026-08-26 a mutation removed a guard from a shell\nscript that performs real deletion, a test exec'd that script with an empty path\nvariable, and the glob expanded to `rm -rf /*` — destroying `/opt/homebrew` and every\nHomebrew-installed app in `/Applications` on a contributor workstation. Only a\n60-second subprocess timeout stopped it; `/Users` was still ahead of it in the target\nlist.\n\nScope is the **OS sandbox only**. The static lint gate that was bundled here was split\nto `6818-615f-555e-4bb9` per this ticket's plan review (finding G5).\n\n## Which subprocess actually matters\n\n`execute_shard()` spawns four subprocesses via `_run()`:\n\n| Line | Subprocess | Executes shard test code? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 579 | baseline `pytest` | **Yes** — unmutated |\n| 583 | `mutmut run --max-children 1` | **Yes — this is the causal path** |\n| 595 | `mutmut results --all true` | No — reporting only |\n| 612 | `mutmut show <mutant>` | No — reporting only |\n\nThe incident occurred under **`mutmut run`**, which executes the *mutated* code.\nSandboxing only the baseline `pytest` would leave the causal path exposed and reproduce\nthe incident exactly. Both test-executing subprocesses are in scope; the two reporting\nsubprocesses are not. `execute_shard()` has exactly two callers, both in\n`mutation_gate.py` (lines 697, 698 — the `base` and `head` runs).\n\n## Why source-tree isolation cannot help\n\ncargo-mutants, mutmut, StrykerJS, PIT and mull all isolate the source tree; **none\nisolates runtime effects.** A copied tree does nothing when mutated code calls\n`rm -rf /` — the syscall still lands. cargo-mutants documents the hazard\n(https://mutants.rs/cautions.html): *\"it might modify existing deletion paths in your\ncode… could potentially delete unintended directories… Run in an isolated environment\n(container, CI, or VM).\"* No mutation tool ships OS-level sandboxing; it is the\ncaller's job. And because the harness string-substitutes the artifact under test, any\nguard **inside** that artifact can be mutated away — only an outside layer is\nload-bearing.\n\n## macOS mechanism: verified, not asserted\n\n`sandbox-exec` is deprecated by Apple, so its enforcement is a load-bearing external\ncapability. **Measured on this host (macOS 26.5.2, `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec`)** with a\nprofile of `(allow default)` + `(deny file-write*)` + an allow-list subpath:\n\n| Probe | Result |\n|---|---|\n| write outside the allow-list | **DENIED** — `Operation not permitted`, file not created |\n| write inside the allow-list | succeeded, file created |\n| `rm -rf <protected>/*` (the incident's shape) | **DENIED** — target survived |\n\nDeprecation is a future risk, not a current gap; the fail-closed posture below means a\nfuture breakage surfaces as an abort rather than a silent unsandboxed run.\n\n## Linux mechanism: bubblewrap only\n\n`bwrap` is the committed Linux mechanism. The previously-considered\n`unshare --map-root-user --mount` fallback is **dropped**: `unshare --mount` creates a\nprivate mount namespace but does not deny writes without a read-only remount, and\nunprivileged `CLONE_NEWUSER` is disabled on many hardened hosts — so it could probe as\npresent yet fail to enforce, which is worse than no sandbox because it looks safe.\nWithout `bwrap`, Linux fails closed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Both shard-test-executing subprocesses in `execute_shard()` — the baseline\n      `pytest` (line 579) **and** the `mutmut run` mutant test-run (line 583) — are\n      wrapped in an OS-enforced sandbox denying filesystem writes outside an explicit\n      allow-list (scratch tree, pytest basetemp, the venv, `/dev/null`). Verified by\n      `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`.\n- [ ] The enforcement test trips the deny under **`mutmut run`** specifically, not only\n      under the baseline `pytest`, since `mutmut run` is the path the incident took.\n- [ ] The sandbox demonstrably DENIES: a probe writing outside the allow-list exits\n      non-zero and the file is absent afterward; the same probe inside the allow-list\n      succeeds. Asserting wrapper argv alone does not satisfy this — the test observes\n      enforcement, not construction.\n- [ ] macOS uses Seatbelt `sandbox-exec` with a generated profile, reproducing the\n      three probes measured above. Verified by `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`\n      on a macOS host.\n- [ ] Linux uses `bwrap`, probed at runtime via `shutil.which`. No `unshare` fallback.\n      Verified by a test asserting the probe selects `bwrap` when present and returns\n      \"unavailable\" when absent.\n- [ ] With no sandbox mechanism available the run ABORTS non-zero with a message naming\n      `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED`. Verified by a test stubbing the mechanism\n      absent and asserting that literal appears in stderr.\n- [ ] `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED=1` waives the sandbox and logs at WARNING; a\n      silent unsandboxed fallback is rejected. Verified by asserting the WARNING record\n      names the same literal.\n- [ ] Enforcement is an OS facility invoked in-process, never a CI-provider feature, and\n      works from a developer laptop with no CI involved (`project.portability`).\n- [ ] `HOME` is set to a non-existent path for both sandboxed subprocesses so adjacent\n      home-directory writes fail loudly. Verified by asserting the child's `HOME`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py` — enforcement under both the baseline\n  and `mutmut run`, mechanism probing, fail-closed abort, opt-out WARNING, and `HOME`.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py` — the existing harness suite still passes\n  (it monkeypatches `execute_shard` at lines 489, 540, 948).\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`,\n  `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` — all green.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Every acceptance criterion above is checked by the command it names.\n- The sandbox logic lands in its own module: `mutation_gate.py` is **762 LOC against\n  the 800 hard cap**, so it cannot absorb this inline.\n- Operator note documents `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED` and the risk of setting it.\n\n## Platform scope\n\nPOSIX only. `pyproject.toml:37-38` classifies the project `Operating System :: POSIX`\nand `Operating System :: MacOS :: MacOS X`; the CI matrix\n(`_build-and-test.yml:311`) is `[ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]`. Windows is out of scope\nbecause it is not a supported platform.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled from the 2026-08-26 incident. Two other leaks found the same day —\ngate-snapshot accumulation (`a37c-d55c-72c3-439b`) and orphaned CPU load generators\n(`f3d9-ecd8-df72-4fa1`) — are unrelated in cause and tracked separately.\n\nPrior plan-review rounds on this ticket caught three real defects, all corrected above:\nthe `mutmut run` scoping (G6), the unnamed opt-out variable (E2), and the unverified\n`unshare` write-deny claim (T1/T3)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787799969454261001,"uuid":"c37c172a-8180-4857-9b92-ee34c63bc0aa"}